  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 343 AM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\n- Storms possible today across Southern Lower Michigan\n\nThe focus today is on the warm front to our south which stretches\nfrom portions of Iowa into Indiana. A low level jet max at 850mb\nwill work eastward down the warm front this morning which should\nbring continued development to convection near and north of the\nfront off to our west. A marked increase is convective coverage\nhas been noted in the past 1-2 hours from the MI/IN border region\nthrough Southern Wisconsin to far Southeast South Dakota. All of\nthis activity is working eastward with the mid level flow towards\nSouthern Lake Michigan. MUCAPE values are significant even at this\nhour with the RAP indicating 2000-5000 j/kg. Given a low level jet\nforcing the activity and a large reservoir of instability the\nactivity will no doubt continue/expand. Agree with both the SPC\nday 1 outlook and machine learning guidance like NadoCast\nindicating the far southwest corner of the state stand the best\nchance at severe weather today. Areas to the south and west of\nGrand Rapids will be the area of concern between 14Z and 21Z.\nWinds looks to be the main threat. \n\n- Less confidence in storms on Wednesday\n\nWednesday we will remain under zonal upper flow with what looks to\nbe convectively driven vorticity maxima working through the area,\nat least in the GFS. The SPC day 2 outlook shows general thunder\nto marginal risk levels across the forecast area. We seem to lack\na trigger on Wednesday with no major shortwave aloft, no low level\njet to speak of aimed at the region and a surface warm front that\nwill likely remain situated in Illinois and Indiana. Given MUCAPE\nvalues in excess of 2,000 j/kg we have thunder in the forecast. \n\n- Drier weather settles in for the latter half of work week\n\nSurface high pressure gradually builds in and takes over our\nweather for Thursday and Friday. BUFKIT overviews look dry for the\nmost part from Wednesday night through Friday night. High\ntemperatures actually trend back into the 70s from Friday into\nSaturday.",
